Chalazion/Hordeolum (Stye)
Opthalmology & ENT
Focal eyelid nodule, Tender/painless swelling, Localized erythema

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-011
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, progressively painful eye swelling causing complete closure of the eyelid and functional visual impairment.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified an acute, highly tender, and rapidly progressive unilateral eyelid swelling causing mechanical ptosis and severe localized pain. Urgent home assessment was essential to definitively rule out preseptal or orbital cellulitis, which represent immediate sight and life-threatening emergencies, and to initiate rapid localized therapy to prevent abscess formation and extensive superficial tissue necrosis prior to morning review.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My eyelid swelled up like a golf ball over the last twelve hours, and every time I blink, it feels like I am being stabbed with a hot needle."
History of Present Illness: Sudden onset of a focal, palpable, and acutely erythematous nodule at the anterior margin of the right upper eyelid commencing yesterday evening. The swelling progressed rapidly overnight, becoming intensely tender and causing significant localized eyelid edema that now partially obscures vision. Patient reports a persistent foreign body sensation as the inflamed tissue rubs the cornea. Denies trauma, fevers, or deep aching retro-orbital pain.
Functional Status: Independent, but visually limited on the right side due to mechanical closure.
Social/Safety Concerns: Anxious about the rapid onset, living alone.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 88 | Temp: 37.1 | RR: 14 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, guarding the right eye, blinking cautiously.
Physical Exam: Right upper eyelid demonstrates marked, circumscribed erythema and localized edema centered around a focal, highly tender, palpable 4mm nodule at the lash line. A discrete, yellow purulent punctum is visible at the apex of the lesion pointing outward. Extraocular movements are full and painless. No proptosis. The conjunctiva exhibits mild reactive injection, but the cornea is completely clear to gross inspection. Visual acuity is intact when the lid is manually elevated. No preauricular lymphadenopathy.
Environmental Observations: Standard home environment, no obvious hazards.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Nil regular medications. No known allergies.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute External Hordeolum (Stye) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Preseptal Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Swelling and erythema being strictly circumscribed to the focal nodule, rather than diffusely involving the entire upper and lower eyelid or extending beyond the orbital rim.

Orbital Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Full, painless extraocular movements, normal visual acuity, and total absence of proptosis or systemic fever.

Chalazion - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The acute, highly inflammatory, and exquisitely painful presentation with a visible purulent punctum, whereas a chalazion is typically chronic, deeper, and painless.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with rapid-onset, severe, and disabling eyelid swelling. Urgent, direct clinical evaluation was categorically required to differentiate a localized eyelash follicle abscess (hordeolum) from rapidly spreading periorbital infections (preseptal/orbital cellulitis). The physical examination definitively localized the pathology to the anterior lid margin without post-septal involvement. Because of the extreme mechanical discomfort and risk of spontaneous, uncontrolled rupture, immediate physical intervention via localized heat application and topical antibiotics was necessary to promote safe, controlled drainage and relieve the acute mechanical distress during the after-hours period.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed topical Chloramphenicol 1% eye ointment to be applied to the lid margin four times daily for 5 days.
Positive Behavioral Change: To accelerate resolution and safely evacuate the micro-abscess, the patient must apply a hot, moist compress for exactly 10 minutes, four times a day. The immediate localized relief from the heat creates a positive feedback loop, directly encouraging strict compliance with the mechanical therapy required to melt the inspissated secretions and promote natural drainage without dangerous manual squeezing.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3-5 days if the lesion does not spontaneously drain or if swelling diffuses.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the redness rapidly spreads over the eyebrow or cheek, if moving the eye becomes painful, or if double vision develops.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; assumes uncomplicated localized infection.
Clinical Course: The swelling will likely localize further and discharge a small amount of pus over the next 48 hours, rapidly relieving the pain.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not squeeze or pop the lump under any circumstances, as this can force the infection backward into the brain. If the whole eyelid suddenly blows up or moving the eye hurts, go straight to the hospital.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A stye is a small bacterial abscess at the root of an eyelash. The hot compresses draw the pus to the surface to burst naturally, and the antibiotic ointment prevents the bacteria from spreading to the rest of the eye.
Actionable Steps: Apply the hot compress for 10 minutes QID. Smear the ointment directly onto the swollen lash line. Discard all old eye makeup immediately.

